= Bias–variance tradeoff =

== Overview ==
The '''bias–variance tradeoff''' is a framework for analyzing prediction error by separating systematic error from sensitivity to variation in the training sample.

Under squared-error regression and standard assumptions, expected prediction error can be decomposed into bias, variance, and irreducible noise.

== Bias ==
Bias measures systematic deviation between the learner's average prediction and the target relationship.

High bias can arise when the model class, features, regularization, or fitting assumptions cannot represent relevant structure.

== Variance ==
Variance measures how much the learned prediction changes across different training samples from the same data-generating process.

High variance indicates sensitivity to finite-sample variation.

== Classical tradeoff ==
In many classical settings, increasing flexibility can reduce bias while increasing variance.

This yields the familiar picture of an intermediate complexity with best expected test error.

That picture is useful but not universal.

== Modern qualification ==
Overparameterized models can interpolate training data and still generalize well.

Belkin et al. described '''double descent''', in which test error can rise near an interpolation threshold and then fall again as model capacity increases.

Therefore:

<pre>
bias–variance analysis ≠ universal single U-shaped complexity curve
</pre>

The decomposition and the empirical complexity curve are related but not identical claims.

